WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:09.940
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:12.430
So we continue our discussion
of nuclear physics.

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:15.880
And in this lecture, we talk
about the nuclear force.

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:21.550
Now, in the last lecture we saw
that nuclei are bound together,

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:23.680
and we were able
to calculate using

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:28.690
an empirical model the binding
energy of various nuclei.

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:31.450
Now, the question
remains, what is actually

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:34.900
binding those nuclei together?

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:37.990
If you remember, I mean, in
the first weeks and months

00:00:37.990 --> 00:00:42.310
of this lecture, we looked and
discussed various interactions

00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:43.930
between elementary particles.

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:46.540
We saw the electromagnetic
interactions,

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:49.120
the weak interaction, and
the strong interactions.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.670
There was no discussion
of the nuclear interaction

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:52.750
or the nuclear force.

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:54.230
Now, what is this?

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:56.440
We have seen that
the strong force acts

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:58.900
between quarks and hadrons.

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:01.240
For example, we have
discussed at length

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:08.010
the pion, the pion,
which is made up of an up

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:09.030
quark and a down quark.

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:11.820
And those are held
together or bound together

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:12.860
via the strong force.

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:15.330
And we looked also at
the structure of a proton

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:17.350
and the structure of a neutron.

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:21.210
Now, the nuclear force is
the residual interaction

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:24.580
between the quarks localized
in different hadrons.

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:28.230
So the interactions between
protons with this up quark here

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:33.470
and the down quark here
in a different nucleon.

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:35.020
You can already
understand that it

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:37.600
will be difficult to
have a full understanding

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:38.920
of the nuclear force.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:39.850
Why?

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:42.730
Because there's many
quarks involved,

00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:47.060
and there's many
protons and neutrons

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:48.980
involved in this process.

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:52.220
So what we find later
that we can describe

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:54.080
this using a mean
field approach,

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:59.710
a mean field of forces between
the particles involved.

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:01.220
So what is the
experimental status?

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:02.915
Our understanding
of the nuclear force

00:02:02.915 --> 00:02:07.400
is based on various kinds
of experimental information.

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:10.850
The first comes from
nucleon-nucleon (proton-proton,

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:12.920
neutron-neutron, and
proton-neutron) scattering

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:14.090
experiments.

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:16.760
And some of those
experiments have the benefit

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:20.090
of using spin-polarized
projectiles, for example

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:23.810
a polarized electron
being used to probe

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:27.620
the structure of a nucleus.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:30.020
Nuclear binding energies,
we've seen those again,

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:31.970
and the precision
measurement of masses,

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:36.350
they give us insight, especially
useful for the light nuclei.

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:38.210
And the nuclear
structure information

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:42.830
such as energies, energy
levels, spin, parities,

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:45.950
magnetic and quadrupole
moments, again,

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:48.380
especially of the light nuclei.

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:51.530
And there's many more to
be named in more detail.

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:53.630
But conceptually, those
are the three kinds

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:57.130
of pieces of
information we have.

00:02:57.130 --> 00:03:00.760
The experiment-- those
experimental results indicate

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:04.390
that the nuclear force depends
on the distance between

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:06.490
the interacting nucleons--

00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:08.000
this is the radial part--

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.660
how far apart are
those nucleons?

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:13.200
And also, the spin and angular
momentum of the interacting

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:13.930
nucleon.

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:18.460
There seems to be a spin-orbit
and also a tensor part

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:22.480
when it comes to
the nuclear force.

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:25.610
It is also interesting to
note-- and we'll talk about this

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:26.890
more--

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:29.500
there doesn't seem
to be any indication

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:32.620
that the nuclear force depends
on the type of nucleon,

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:37.420
whether or not it's a proton or
a neutron in the interaction.

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:39.250
So that's charge independence.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:44.700
So looking at the
radial part of this,

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:48.200
nuclear force is
short range, which

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:51.140
implies it vanishes
for distances longer

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:53.040
than about 2 femtometers.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:56.630
So it basically vanishes
in this area here.

00:03:56.630 --> 00:03:58.340
And the nuclear
force is strongly

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:04.940
repulsive for distances shorter
than about 0.5, in this area,

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:06.440
femtometers.

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:09.170
You can understand the
repulsiveness by the fact that

00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:14.540
you cannot really push or press
an existing nucleon further

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:16.529
than its actual radius.

00:04:16.529 --> 00:04:19.310
You cannot compress
them further.

00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:26.960
This is also kind of apparent
in the liquid-drop model,

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:29.990
where we discussed, you
know, the volume term,

00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:31.840
and the volume is not--

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:33.890
cannot be compressed further.

00:04:33.890 --> 00:04:37.530
On the other hand, you saw that
there is short-range distance,

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:43.310
in fact, because we find this
linearity with the mass number

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:46.090
in the binding energies.

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:46.590
Right.

00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:47.673
So here are the arguments.

00:04:47.673 --> 00:04:50.460
The binding energies per nucleus
which is roughly constant,

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:53.190
indicates that the nucleons
and nuclei interact only

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:54.840
in their immediate neighbors.

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:57.330
Otherwise, it would have
an A squared term or an A

00:04:57.330 --> 00:04:59.970
times A minus 1 term in there.

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:02.320
And then the measurement of
distances between the nuclei

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:04.920
at which nuclear
reactions start to occur,

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:08.820
those are in the order of
1 to 2 femtometers larger

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:14.300
than the corresponding radii.

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:15.890
The nuclear densities
which are only

00:05:15.890 --> 00:05:17.270
slightly smaller
than the nucleon

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:20.330
densities, indicating
very dense packing.

00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:22.940
So again, they are already
very densely packed.

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:26.870
You cannot push
them much further.

00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:33.800
On the spin-orbit
force, here that's

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:36.060
an area where we could
go into much more detail.

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:38.580
But for this introductory
class, we don't.

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:39.800
We will not.

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:45.020
The scattering of spin-polarized
nucleons or other spin nuclei

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:47.180
particles allows
us to understand

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:49.160
that nuclear force
has a component which

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:51.680
depends on the spin
and the angular

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:56.350
momentum of the interaction.

00:05:56.350 --> 00:05:57.430
Here's a fun fact.

00:05:57.430 --> 00:06:00.760
So the charge independence
nuclear forces,

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:02.955
meaning that it doesn't
really-- the nuclear force

00:06:02.955 --> 00:06:04.580
doesn't really depend
on whether or not

00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:06.280
there's protons and neutrons.

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:07.150
And you can--

00:06:07.150 --> 00:06:10.750
I could ask you, you know,
would you have expected this?

00:06:10.750 --> 00:06:12.370
Would you have
expected that there

00:06:12.370 --> 00:06:13.720
is a dependence on the charge?

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:16.480
And the answer
should have been no.

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:19.405
Because we just learned
that the nuclear force is

00:06:19.405 --> 00:06:20.905
a remnant of the
strong interaction,

00:06:20.905 --> 00:06:24.440
the strong interaction doesn't
know about electric charges.

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:28.180
So the answer needs to be yes.

00:06:28.180 --> 00:06:30.850
The charge independence
of nuclear force

00:06:30.850 --> 00:06:33.820
implies that electromagnetic
effects are eliminated

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:39.220
in this scattering, meaning
that when you measure aspects

00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:43.030
of the nuclear force, you
have to be aware of the fact

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:45.040
that there is
electromagnetic interactions,

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:47.470
and you have to kind
of try to get them out.

00:06:47.470 --> 00:06:50.050
And that can be done
by, for example,

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:53.380
comparing scatterings of
protons and protons, protons

00:06:53.380 --> 00:06:55.360
and neutrons,
neutrons and neutrons.

00:06:55.360 --> 00:06:58.355
If you do a comparison
carefully and subtract out

00:06:58.355 --> 00:06:59.980
the electromagnetic
effect, then you'll

00:06:59.980 --> 00:07:06.550
see that the force is indeed
independent of the charge.

00:07:06.550 --> 00:07:08.150
We can make use of this.

00:07:08.150 --> 00:07:12.430
So there's a lot of
information behind,

00:07:12.430 --> 00:07:16.990
or experimental techniques
which can make use of the fact

00:07:16.990 --> 00:07:20.500
that nuclear forces
are charge independent.

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:26.690
What you can, for example, do is
study so-called mirror nuclei.

00:07:26.690 --> 00:07:28.120
Those are the ones
where basically

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:33.440
revert the N and the
Z for the same A.

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:35.450
So they're basically
mirrors of themselves

00:07:35.450 --> 00:07:38.390
in terms of mirroring the
protons and the neutrons.

00:07:38.390 --> 00:07:44.060
Examples are helium-3
and tritium, for example.

00:07:44.060 --> 00:07:51.200
And those, then, allow you to
study in detail those effects.

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:55.290
So this heavy mirror nuclei
inherit heavy mirror nuclei,

00:07:55.290 --> 00:07:56.930
the breaking effect--

00:07:56.930 --> 00:07:58.680
the effect breaking
charge independence

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:03.120
of the nuclear force are strong,
and the similar does not hold.

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:04.170
Good.

00:08:04.170 --> 00:08:06.630
One example where you can
study is if, for example,

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:14.830
one of those mirrors is
radioactive or unstable nuclei.

00:08:14.830 --> 00:08:18.660
You can study the properties
of the unstable nuclei

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:22.120
by looking in detail at
the mirrored nucleus.

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:25.470
So that's one of the common
and interesting ways to study

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:29.100
radioactive nuclei, where you
cannot just simply take them,

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:30.980
excite them, and
study the properties.

00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:35.030
They simply decay too
fast in some cases.

00:08:35.030 --> 00:08:37.159
Here is a table where
you see this effect.

00:08:37.159 --> 00:08:41.544
You see the comparison
between the nuclei,

00:08:41.544 --> 00:08:43.030
this mirror nuclei here.

00:08:43.030 --> 00:08:44.440
There's four pairs.

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:47.290
And you see that the binding
energy, the net binding

00:08:47.290 --> 00:08:50.660
energy after removing
the Coulomb term,

00:08:50.660 --> 00:08:52.810
is very much the same
between those groups,

00:08:52.810 --> 00:08:55.150
those mirror groups of nuclei.

00:08:55.150 --> 00:08:57.070
And this table here
or this diagram

00:08:57.070 --> 00:09:01.750
shows you excitation energies
for two mirror nuclei.

00:09:01.750 --> 00:09:07.060
And you see that the energy
levels are pretty much on par.

00:09:07.060 --> 00:09:08.650
Without going into
any detail, they're

00:09:08.650 --> 00:09:11.880
on par between those
two mirrored nuclei.

